Let me re-analyze the code:

Looking at `lib/index.js`:

```javascript
opts.string.forEach(key => {
    opts.string = opts.string.concat(opts.alias[key] = opts.alias[key] || []);
});
```

This iterates over the current `opts.string` and for each key, adds its aliases to `opts.string`.

But when defaults processing happens:
```javascript
if (defaults) {
    for (k in opts.default) {
        opts.alias[k] = opts.alias[k] || [];
        (opts[typeof opts.default[k]] || []).push(k);
    }
}
```

This adds `k` (the key itself) to the appropriate type array, but NOT the aliases of `k`.

The fix should be: in the defaults block, also push the aliases of each default key to the appropriate type array.

Let me look at the code more carefully:

```javascript
if (defaults) {
    for (k in opts.default) {
        opts.alias[k] = opts.alias[k] || [];
        (opts[typeof opts.default[k]] || []).push(k);
    }
}
```

The fix: also push `opts.alias[k]` elements to the type array:

```javascript
if (defaults) {
    for (k in opts.default) {
        opts.alias[k] = opts.alias[k] || [];
        (opts[typeof opts.default[k]] || []).push(k);
        // Also push aliases
        opts.alias[k].forEach(alias => {
            (opts[typeof opts.default[k]] || []).push(alias);
        });
    }
}
```

Or more concisely, I can concatenate the aliases to the type array in one operation.

